# --------------------------
# Authentication (Required if REQUIRE_AUTH=true)
# --------------------------
PROXY_USER=user
PROXY_PASSWORD=password
#   The client calls the same setting PROXY_PASS. The server accepts that name
#   too, with a warning in the log, so a file copied from the client side does
#   not turn into a server that refuses to start.

# Multi-account mode: accounts, quotas and expiry dates in a JSON file.
# Passwords are stored as Argon2id hashes; the KDF runs once per password, not
# once per connection (see docs/benchmarks/argon2-cost.md), so this mode costs
# the same as PROXY_USER/PROXY_PASSWORD in steady state.
# USERS_FILE=/etc/s5core/users.json
#   An account may also carry "tunnel_key": 32 random bytes in base64
#   (openssl rand -base64 32), independent of the password. A client given
#   that key as OBFS_MEMBER_KEY is recognised by the obfuscated transport
#   before the SOCKS5 handshake and is never asked for a password there.
#   See docs/benchmarks/roster.md.
# KDF_MEMORY_BUDGET_MB=0
#   How much memory password checking may use at once. Argon2id asks for
#   64 MiB per run, and who runs it is decided by whoever opens connections,
#   so the bound is on memory rather than on connections. 0 is the default
#   of 256 MiB, which is four checks at once plus a queue; past both, a check
#   is refused rather than run, and that shows up as
#   s5core_auth_verifications_total{path="overloaded"}.
# TRAFFIC_FLUSH_INTERVAL=60s
#   How often the traffic counters are written to USERS_FILE. Quotas are also
#   checked while a session runs, so this does not decide how long a user can
#   keep downloading past their limit - see README, "Quotas and validity dates
#   during a session". SIGTERM flushes as well, so a clean restart loses none.

# --------------------------
# General Server Settings
# --------------------------
# PROXY_PORT=1080
# PROXY_LISTEN_IP=0.0.0.0
# REQUIRE_AUTH=true

# --------------------------
# Access Control Lists (ACL)
# --------------------------
# Regex allow-list for destinations. Anchored to the whole destination unless
# the pattern anchors itself - which is decided by parsing it, so the ^ in a
# class like [^.] does not count. A name is matched without regard to case,
# an address as the literal the client sent, and the rule is checked before
# DNS - a refused destination is never resolved.
#   ALLOWED_DEST_FQDN=example\.com            -> example.com only
#   ALLOWED_DEST_FQDN=(^|\.)example\.com$     -> example.com and subdomains
#   ALLOWED_DEST_FQDN=[^.]+\.example\.com     -> one label deep, still anchored
#   ALLOWED_DEST_FQDN=203\.0\.113\.\d+        -> that address range, as literals
# ALLOWED_DEST_FQDN=
# Client addresses allowed to connect, comma-separated. Single addresses only,
# v4 or v6: a network in CIDR form is refused by name and the server does not
# start, because an unreadable list would otherwise become an empty one, and an
# empty list is no restriction at all.
#   ALLOWED_IPS=203.0.113.7,2001:db8::1
# ALLOWED_IPS=

# --------------------------
# Timeouts & Limits
# --------------------------
# READ_TIMEOUT=30s
# WRITE_TIMEOUT=30s
#   Idle timeouts for the relay phase, refreshed by every byte.
# HANDSHAKE_TIMEOUT=15s
#   Absolute budget for the setup phase: version byte, authentication and the
#   reply to CONNECT. It is not refreshed by traffic, so a client that sends
#   one byte per second is dropped instead of holding a slot forever.
#   It is also what closes an obfuscated connection that never authenticated:
#   the server holds such a connection and says nothing rather than hanging
#   up, so that a probe cannot tell a refused frame from bytes that never
#   became one (docs/design/decoy.md). Lowering it lowers what a scanner costs.
#   Neither timeout applies to the UDP-over-TCP tunnel (0x83) or to the TCP
#   connection of a UDP association: those are silent by design and would be
#   torn down by an idle timeout while perfectly healthy.
# DIAL_TIMEOUT=10s
#   Budget for reaching the destination - resolution plus connect - carved out
#   of HANDSHAKE_TIMEOUT. A destination that never answers now releases the
#   slot here instead of holding it for the whole setup budget, and such
#   connections are visible as s5core_sessions{region="protocol",
#   state="dialing"} rather than hidden in the total.
# FRAME_TIMEOUT=10s
#   How long a half-read obfuscation frame may stay half-read. It applies only
#   between a frame header and its body, so a peer that stops mid-frame is
#   bounded while a tunnel that is legitimately silent is not. The plain
#   listener has no frames and ignores it.
# QUOTA_GRACE=5s
#   How long a session whose account just ran out may drain what is already in
#   flight. Nothing new goes to the destination either way; 0 ends the session
#   where the quota is noticed.
# MAX_CONNECTIONS=10000

# --------------------------
# Security Limits (Fail2Ban)
# --------------------------
# FAIL2BAN_RETRIES=5
#   Failed attempts from one source (IPv6 grouped by /64) before it is banned.
# FAIL2BAN_TIME=5m
#   How long a source stays banned, and the window of the per-account counter.
#   The account counter never refuses the owner: it raises an alert and adds a
#   short delay, because locking the account is what the attacker wanted.

# --------------------------
# Observability
# --------------------------
# LOG_LEVEL=info
#   One of: debug, info, warn, error. Applies to both s5core and s5client.
#   The level can be changed on a running process: edit the environment and
#   send SIGHUP - no restart, no dropped connections.
#   debug turns on protocol diagnostics (handshake phase, why a connection was
#   refused). Turn it on for an incident and turn it back off afterwards:
#   see docs/design/observability-policy.md for what is allowed in logs.
# LOG_LEVEL_FILE=
#   Optional path to a file containing a single level word. It takes precedence
#   over LOG_LEVEL and is what makes SIGHUP useful on a live server: the
#   environment of a running process cannot be changed from outside, a file can.
#   Without it, SIGUSR1 still toggles debug on and off with no configuration.
# METRICS_PORT=8080
# METRICS_BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1
#   WARNING: Do not expose /metrics to the public internet without a reverse proxy
#   or firewall. Set to 0.0.0.0 only inside a trusted network or VPN.

# --------------------------
# Obfuscation (Server & Client)
# --------------------------
# OBFS_ENABLED=false
# OBFS_PORT=27015
#   Pick a port outside the 443 family. There a middlebox expects a TLS
#   ClientHello and this transport sends high-entropy bytes from the first one,
#   so the port chosen to look ordinary is where the traffic stands out. For a
#   tunnel on 443 use WS_ENABLED, which speaks real TLS.
#   The built-in default is still 1443, for deployments that already use it.
# OBFS_PSK=your-32-byte-pre-shared-key-here
# OBFS_MAX_PADDING=256
#   Padding added to the frames THIS side sends. The receiver reads the
#   payload length from the decrypted header, so the two sides are free to
#   differ. Only OBFS_PSK has to match. Padding never takes more than half
#   of what a frame can carry, whatever is set here.
# OBFS_MTU=1400
#   Largest frame THIS side puts on the wire, header and tag included. Writes
#   are cut into equal parts that fit it, and the connection's buffers are
#   sized from it. It does not have to match the peer: each side reads
#   whatever frame length the other declares.
# KEEPALIVE_MIN=0s
# KEEPALIVE_MAX=0s
#   Server-side keepalive, off by default. The client is the end that holds an
#   idle path open, and a server sending frames to every idle connection adds
#   traffic that buys nothing. Turn it on when the clients are not s5client.
# OBFS_REPLAY_WINDOW=10000
#   How many session salts the server remembers, across all obfuscated
#   listeners, so that a recorded connection cannot be sent again on a new
#   socket. One entry costs 16 bytes plus map overhead, so the default is a
#   few hundred kilobytes for the whole server - the old per-connection nonce
#   window cost 82 KB per connection and could not see across connections at
#   all. Once the history is full the oldest salt is forgotten, so this is the
#   number of connections a replay has to outlive to work. Set it to 0 to
#   switch the check off. Client-side it means nothing: the client is the end
#   that draws the prologue.
# OBFS_PROLOGUE=
#   Client only: printable or raw. Leave it empty (printable). The prologue
#   is 32 uniformly random bytes, which is exactly what a policy that blocks
#   fully encrypted traffic looks for; printable sends it base64-encoded with
#   a pad whose length comes from the session secret, so the connection opens
#   with 43-63 printable characters. Measured on a filtering path: 78
#   connections with it, zero failures; 48 without it, 45 failures. The
#   server accepts both without being told, so a fleet updates server first,
#   clients after. See docs/field/stealth.md and section 3.5 of
#   docs/veil-spec.md.
# OBFS_SPLIT_OPENING=
#   Client only: true or false (default false). Puts the opening in a packet
#   of its own ahead of the frames. It buys the other exemption measured on
#   the same path: that filter classifies only first packets of 100 bytes and
#   up, and the opening alone is 43-72 where the whole first write is 125 and
#   up. With a raw prologue, which has no printable exemption, it moved a
#   live tunnel from 14 of 26 connections to 26 of 26, and it costs no round
#   trip (0,193 s to first byte against 0,189 s). It is off by default because
#   a short packet in a fixed place is a shape of its own, and the printable
#   opening already passes this path without adding one. The server needs no
#   setting: it reads the opening out of the stream. See docs/field/stealth.md.
# OBFS_CIPHER=
#   Client only: aes or chacha. Leave it empty. The client then takes AES
#   where the processor has AES instructions and ChaCha20 where it has not,
#   which moves about twelve times more traffic on a router without them.
#   The server accepts both and reads the choice out of the prologue, so
#   this never has to match anything - it is here for measuring the other
#   one. See docs/benchmarks/ciphers.md.
OBFS_NODE_ID=
#   Binds the keys to one node. Both ends must set the same value; it is
#   never sent, so a mismatch fails the way a wrong PSK fails - the server
#   accepts the connection, says nothing and closes it. Empty is itself an
#   identity: a client with a name cannot reach a server without one.
#   What it buys: a connection recorded against this node is worthless
#   against any other, so each node needs only its own replay history.
#   What it costs: anycast, and moving a client between nodes without
#   editing its configuration.
# OBFS_ACCEPT_NODE_IDS=
#   Server only. Comma-separated names this node still answers to while
#   clients are being migrated to a new one. Each costs one HMAC, and only
#   on a connection that did not match the first name. Remove the old name
#   when the clients are gone.
# OBFS_MEMBER_ID=
# OBFS_MEMBER_KEY=
#   Client only: the account this client belongs to, and its tunnel_key from
#   the server's users.json. With them the server knows who is calling
#   before the first frame and answers the SOCKS5 handshake without asking
#   for a password. Without them the client uses the shared account. A key
#   the server does not know fails the way a wrong PSK fails - silence, then
#   a closed connection.
# OBFS_REQUIRE_MEMBER_KEY=false
#   Server only. false is the migration setting: clients without a key of
#   their own still connect with the PSK and a password. Turn it on once
#   every client has a key - after that the PSK alone no longer reaches the
#   tunnel.
#
#   Clocks. The prologue is authenticated against the current hour, and the
#   server accepts two hours either side of its own. A machine whose clock
#   is further out than that will dial, get silence and time out; the server
#   logs "Peer clock is out of step" and counts it in
#   s5core_obfs_clock_skew_total.

# --------------------------
# WebSocket Stealth Transport (Server & Client)
# --------------------------
# WS_ENABLED=false
#   Server: TLS -> WebSocket -> obfs -> SOCKS5. Every path except WS_PATH
#   serves a decoy site, so the endpoint looks like an ordinary HTTPS host.

# --- Server side ---
# WS_ADDR=0.0.0.0:443
# WS_CERT_FILE=/path/to/cert.pem
# WS_KEY_FILE=/path/to/key.pem
# One exact literal path: it must start with /, and cannot be /,
# /favicon.ico, end with /, contain whitespace or control characters, a
# query or fragment, percent-encoding, . or .. segments, or the router's
# wildcard syntax ({}). The decoy site serves everything else, and the
# endpoint is compared literally against the request path, so anything
# else is refused at startup rather than when the router sees it.
# WS_PATH=/ws
# WS_SUBPROTOCOL=
# WS_DECOY_UPSTREAM=
#   The site every non-tunnel request is proxied to: status, headers and
#   body come from it, including its own 404 for WS_PATH itself. Empty
#   serves the built-in page, which answers three paths in the same way
#   every time and is weaker cover - see docs/design/decoy.md. http:// or
#   https://, with a host, no query. The upstream is never told who is visiting:
#   X-Forwarded-* headers are removed on the way up.
# TRANSPORT_ADVICE=
#   Told to every client inside its tunnel once it is up: the transport to use
#   from its next connection and the shape to adopt. A bare "ws" or "obfs", or
#   fields separated by spaces or commas:
#     transport=ws min_frame=512 max_frame=2048 jitter_ms=5 padding=128 keepalive=10s-20s
#   The advised transport must be one this server listens on. Empty sends
#   nothing. Read once, at startup: to change it on a running server, use
#   TRANSPORT_ADVICE_FILE. See docs/field/migration.md.
# TRANSPORT_ADVICE_FILE=
#   Path to a file holding one line of the same syntax. Set, it wins over
#   TRANSPORT_ADVICE and is re-read on SIGHUP - that is what makes moving a
#   fleet one edit and one signal rather than a restart, since the environment
#   of a running process cannot be changed from outside it. A missing file
#   means no advice, which is how a recommendation is withdrawn; a file that
#   cannot be read or does not parse leaves the previous advice in force and
#   is logged.

# --- Client side ---
# WS_URL=wss://your-domain.com/ws
# WS_HOST=your-domain.com
# WS_ORIGIN=https://your-domain.com
# WS_USER_AGENT="Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36"
# SERVER_NAME=
#   The SNI to present, and the name the certificate is verified against.
#   Empty falls back to WS_HOST and then to the host in WS_URL, so set this
#   only when the SNI has to differ from the Host header.
# WS_CA_FILE=
#   PEM file with the CA (or the server certificate itself) to trust. It
#   replaces the system roots. Required when the server uses a self-signed
#   certificate: the client verifies the certificate now and will refuse an
#   unknown one instead of talking to whoever answers.
# WS_PIN_SHA256=
#   Comma-separated SHA-256 hashes of the server public key (SPKI), hex,
#   colons optional. Checked in addition to the chain, never instead of it.
#   openssl x509 -in cert.pem -pubkey -noout \
#     | openssl pkey -pubin -outform der | openssl dgst -sha256
# TLS_FINGERPRINT=chrome
#   Client-side only: which browser's TLS Client Hello to imitate. The server
#   ignores it and says so in the log, because setting it there and assuming
#   the traffic is shaped is a mistake that costs nothing to make.

# --- Frame shaping: applies to what this side sends ---
#   The peer reassembles a byte stream, so these do not have to match. Set
#   them on both sides if you want both directions shaped. Every write is cut
#   into at least two frames of a varying count, so the length of an
#   obfuscated frame never reaches the wire as the length of a TLS record.
#   The band is a range to cut to, not a size to pad to: a frame smaller than
#   WS_MIN_FRAME is sent as it is. WS_MAX_FRAME=0 turns shaping off entirely.
# WS_MIN_FRAME=256
# WS_MAX_FRAME=4096
# WS_MAX_JITTER_MS=0

# --------------------------
# S5Client-only Settings
# --------------------------
# CLIENT_LISTEN_ADDR=127.0.0.1:1080
# CLIENT_MAX_CONNECTIONS=1024
#   Bounds active local connections, including handshakes. Excess sockets close.
#   HANDSHAKE_TIMEOUT also bounds the local handshake (15s if non-positive).
# TIMEZONE_CHECK=false
#   Off by default. When true, the client asks ipapi.co which timezone the
#   server address belongs to and warns if the system timezone differs. That
#   request hands a third party the pairing "this client uses this proxy" and
#   is a reliable tell on the wire just before every connection to the proxy.
#   For a one-off check without turning it on: s5client timezone
# DIAL_TIMEOUT=10s
#   How long to wait for the connection to the server to be established.
# HANDSHAKE_TIMEOUT=15s
#   Deadline for the whole tunnel setup: greeting, authentication and the
#   CONNECT reply. Cleared once the tunnel is up, so an established but idle
#   connection is not killed by it. On expiry the application gets a SOCKS5
#   error (0x06) and the log gets one WARN with the destination and the phase.
# SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT=10s
#   How long a shutdown waits for connections that are still carrying traffic.
#   It used to wait without end, so a client asked to stop kept running.
# KEEPALIVE_MIN=10s
# KEEPALIVE_MAX=20s
#   An idle tunnel is dropped by whatever sits on the path. Measured: s5core
#   itself after READ_TIMEOUT (30 s by default, the shortest of them all),
#   nginx after proxy_read_timeout (60 s by default). Published: Cloudflare
#   about 100 s, AWS NAT Gateway 350 s, Azure Load Balancer 4 minutes. The
#   interval must clear the shortest one on your path, which is why the range
#   tops out well under 30 s - see the keepalive table in README.
#   The interval is drawn anew for every frame and suppressed by real traffic,
#   so it does not become a periodic signature; the frame itself is the size of
#   a frame this connection has already sent. Set the minimum to 0 to disable.
# SERVER_ADDR=your-server-ip:27015
# ROUTE_DOMAINS=example.com,*.google.com
# TRANSPORT=auto
#   obfs, ws, or auto. Auto is the configured default (ws when WS_URL is set,
#   obfs otherwise), overridden by the server's TRANSPORT_ADVICE when it sends
#   one, with the other transport tried when the chosen one fails to set up.
#   A pinned value is used regardless of both. ws needs WS_URL. A client that
#   should be able to move has both SERVER_ADDR and WS_URL set in advance: the
#   advice names a transport, not an address.
# TRANSPORT_COOLDOWN=5m
#   How long a transport that failed to set up is rested while the other one
#   carries the traffic. 0 turns the switch off.
# OBFS_FORMAT=auto
#   v1 (docs/veil-spec.md), legacy (the format before it, for a server that
#   has not been updated), or auto: v1 first, legacy for OBFS_FORMAT_REPROBE
#   after a server accepted the connection and did not answer, then v1 again.
#   Set v1 once every server is updated - the legacy format is detectable.
#   Scheduled for removal: docs/field/migration.md.
# OBFS_FORMAT_REPROBE=10m
